WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:06.958
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.320
PROFESSOR: I have with me this afternoon John White,

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:19.050
who is the State Superintendent of Education for the state of Louisiana.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.000
He was the Deputy Chancellor for the New York City school systems.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.000
So he's had a lot of experience with a broad range of organizations

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.680
and positions in education.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.850
And currently, he's the Chair of the Chiefs

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.050
for Change, an organization of state chief officers

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:44.030
who are committed to reform in American education.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.340
And I'm very pleased to have the opportunity

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.680
to talk with him today about the amazing developments that

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.540
have been occurring in Louisiana.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.870
Louisiana has always been a low performing state.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:01.640
But in recent years, under John White's leadership,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.620
this state has made more progress than any other state in the United States,

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.670
according to the National Assessment of Educational Progress.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.600
And it all starts, perhaps, with Katrina,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.210
the great hurricane in New Orleans.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.760
Because that washed away the preexisting school system.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.760
So John, that's what I'd like to really ask you about.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.560
How did it happen that a new school system was built out of--

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.410
shall I say-- the ashes of-- or at least the mud and the sludge

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.512
of the Katrina storm.

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:39.720
JOHN WHITE: A great tragedy--

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.100
of course, we call it a man-made tragedy in New Orleans.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.249
But prior to Katrina, New Orleans, by any measure,

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:49.790
was the lowest performing urban school system in the country.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.620
It was the second lowest performing school system

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.940
in the second lowest performing state in America.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.270
When you have to vacate your entire city for weeks and months,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.180
and sometimes even years, for many of our people, you have no students.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.320
When you have no students, you have no revenue for a school board.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.850
And when that happened, the school board in New Orleans in 2005,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.390
tragically, severed its contract with its teachers.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.450
And the state stepped in and said, well, we

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.630
will then take over the governance of every school in New Orleans, which

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.860
is struggling academically.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.500
And that was nearly 90% of the schools.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.080
Over time, the state transitioned those schools

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.740
from being run by the state to being operated by independent charter school

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:31.400
organizations.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.790
That took many, many years.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:33.890
But it took a decade.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.810
And now we are where we are, with a system

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.764
that is almost exclusively independent charter schools.

00:02:38.764 --> 00:02:41.180
PROFESSOR: Well, actually, you had a little help, I think.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.138
That's what I've heard.

00:02:42.138 --> 00:02:46.160
And maybe you could tell me, were there some outside resources

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.090
that became available, simply because the tragedy captured

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.600
national attention?

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.870
Did foundations come in?

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:53.870
JOHN WHITE: No question.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.260
I mean, New Orleans is a city that is at the heart of the American identity.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.780
It's a city that, whenever you mention, people have smiles across their faces.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.420
And it's also a city of great poverty.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.510
And philanthropic interests know that, from across the country,

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.680
as well as from within Louisiana.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.960
Also, people, in a range of issues, from blight and Urban Development issues,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.810
all the way to education and health issues, came to Louisiana--

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.430
I count myself as one of them--

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.760
to help with what I think of as a reformation,

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.490
but not just of a school system since a storm--

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:29.300
really, of tragedies that have befallen low income people in Louisiana

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.390
and in the South for centuries.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.089
And that's what our project is about.

00:03:33.089 --> 00:03:34.130
But no question about it.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.310
Both people from the inside and people from the outside were part of this.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.268
PROFESSOR: Young people came, too, didn't they?

00:03:39.268 --> 00:03:41.360
Didn't young teachers from around the country

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.440
feel it was sort of their moral obligation to go to New Orleans

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.560
and help out during this time of stress?

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.060
JOHN WHITE: Yeah, I think there was a missionary feel to it.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.550
Certainly, I view it as a moral calling.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:55.910
At the same time, it also gave rise to a whole generation

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:00.770
of New Orleans who had, as is the case in New York or anywhere else,

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.230
existed within a school board establishment that's

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:07.240
very hard to be creative within, and very hard to be upwardly mobile in.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.130
So there are a lot of veteran New Orleans educators

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:13.522
who are now able to run schools that they always wanted to run.

00:04:13.522 --> 00:04:15.980
And they're able to do it in a charter school system, where

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.668
they work for a volunteer board, rather than for an elected

00:04:18.668 --> 00:04:19.709
board and superintendent.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:21.890
PROFESSOR: So this released a lot of internal energy

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.996
as well as bringing in external energy.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:25.620
JOHN WHITE: Yeah, no question about it.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.360
And over the last 10 years, we've been finding our way to,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.940
how do you give life to local voices?

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.920
How do you recruit the right outside voices?

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.960
And how, when you recruit outside voices,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.380
do you make sure that the things that are important to us in New Orleans--

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.210
music, for example, and the arts--

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.910
incredibly important to the identity of people in New Orleans,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.280
and to their upbringings-- how do you make sure

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:49.275
that you never lose those things, even when you have outside talent?

00:04:49.275 --> 00:04:52.400
PROFESSOR: And the federal government helped with some building of schools?

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.474
Was that part of the asset that you were able to work with?

00:04:55.474 --> 00:04:56.640
JOHN WHITE: It doesn't hurt.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:57.306
It doesn't hurt.

00:04:57.306 --> 00:05:01.040
Every child, now, is in a renovated or a fully reconstructed building--

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.840
some amazing buildings.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.530
The school where Ruby Bridges went to school in New Orleans,

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.140
we have totally rebuilt--

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.900
preserved the facade and preserved her classroom.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.440
It's amazing the resources that came into New Orleans

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.700
after that great tragedy.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.790
And of course those were opportunities for the next generation of kids

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.910
as much as they were tragedies for many of the kids who were there.

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.719
We also benefited from an investment in innovation

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:27.010
that the federal government-- the Obama administration,

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.500
rather than the Bush administration, in this case-- made,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.050
to grow high performing charter organizations.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.420
So we've done some good things.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.510
We've certainly had a lot of help along the way.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.912
It's not resources that every city has at their disposal.

00:05:40.912 --> 00:05:43.120
But it's also fair to say that the principles of it--

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.270
independent schools, recruiting talent-- those are things that anybody can do.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.070
PROFESSOR: Well, is there any good evidence that all of this

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.320
has paid off-- that students are actually

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.380
learning more today than they had been in the days

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.882
before all of this renovation took place?

00:05:55.882 --> 00:05:58.090
JOHN WHITE: One of the things we're also blessed with

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.256
is, it wasn't just educators, but it was political scientists and economists

00:06:01.256 --> 00:06:02.250
who came to Louisiana.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.150
They wanted to study this stuff, too.

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:08.440
In fact, Tulane has started a center run by an economist named Doug Harris.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.510
And economists got together and studied the effects, in multiple ways,

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:16.720
of what's happened in Louisiana, and in New Orleans, specifically,

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.340
and have concluded that, statistically, no school system that those researchers

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.730
are aware of has demonstrated the effect on student achievement

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.180
in such a short period of time than have New Orleans schools.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:29.590
I do want to be clear, though.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:31.210
We have a long, long way to go.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:34.990
New Orleans has gone from the bottom of the state in Louisiana

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:37.730
to around the median in the state of Louisiana,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.930
which, given its poverty rate and other things, is an incredible thing.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.390
But it's not nearly at the top of our state.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.310
And our state still performs relatively low in the national rankings.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.820
So we have a long, long way to go.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.350
PROFESSOR: Well, Louisiana has also enacted a school voucher program.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.200
So it's a statewide school voucher program, I think.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:00.340
It has its component in New Orleans, but elsewhere throughout the state.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:01.609
How has that worked out?

00:07:01.609 --> 00:07:04.150
JOHN WHITE: Well, it's shown a lot of good, positive effects.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.810
It's shown positive effects in terms of greater integration of schools.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.900
It's shown positive effects in terms of parent approval.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.810
It's begun to show positive effects in initial research,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.930
in terms of the effect of having greater levels of competition.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.214
So kids, in other schools in proximity to the voucher schools, are doing well.

00:07:22.214 --> 00:07:24.130
And we'll have research coming out soon that I

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.640
think will demonstrate that the voucher schools right now are performing

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.850
on a par with schools in the rest of the system.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.319
That said, for me, it's not about whether it's

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:36.610
a private school, a charter school, or a traditional public school-- it's just,

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:37.630
is it a good school?

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:40.990
My view is, we should measure them all, give parents access to all of them,

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.794
and that the system will be better for parents having that choice, provided

00:07:44.794 --> 00:07:47.710
that we can validate that there's good work going on in those schools.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.501
PROFESSOR: Well, the overall system in Louisiana is moving forward.

00:07:50.501 --> 00:07:52.564
What do you attribute that to?

00:07:52.564 --> 00:07:53.980
JOHN WHITE: It's a simple formula.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.900
This stuff is not rocket science.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.530
But it's actually having access to early childhood education.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:03.100
It's having benchmarks, standards, and assessments, and curriculum.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.160
And curriculum matters.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:04.980
I have to say that.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.870
It's having prepared teachers, who are prepared in the classroom,

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.685
not in the ivory tower--

00:08:09.685 --> 00:08:11.830
a pathway to prosperity for every graduate,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.870
and revitalizing our career and technical education system--

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.077
and then, finally, not being afraid to intervene.

00:08:17.077 --> 00:08:18.910
When things are really going off the rails--

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.570
and typically, in our country, that affects low-income kids of color--

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.200
you can't be afraid to intervene.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.440
And so we're looking at Baton Rouge, and doing work

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.840
in Shreveport and other cities, where there

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.250
are high concentrations of struggling schools in low-income neighborhoods.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.000
That's not something that we can tolerate.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.166
And you have to be willing to do something about it.

00:08:36.166 --> 00:08:39.309
PROFESSOR: So you're the head of Chiefs for Change, which

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:43.419
is trying to introduce reforms throughout the country,

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:46.720
working together with other people who are like you--

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.500
the head of the educational system in that state.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.610
So how can you export what you're learning in Louisiana?

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.689
How does it work when you are working with an organization like that?

00:08:58.689 --> 00:09:01.980
JOHN WHITE: Well, Chiefs for Change is a group of-- there's a handful of us who

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.400
are State Chiefs--

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:08.470
Mississippi, New Mexico, Nevada, Massachusetts, the Washington,

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.510
DC State Superintendent.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.530
So we've got a good mix of state chiefs.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.360
We've also got district chiefs.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.990
In our system-- the fragmented American education system--

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:18.400
you have to have both on board.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.230
You can't have the state and the district

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.410
working consistently at odds with one another.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.970
And so we have huge district superintendents

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.270
that are doing amazing things, from California all the way to Florida,

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:29.420
in our group, too.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.920
We have formed working groups to take on what we think

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.610
are the greatest challenges coming up.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.230
One of them is, how should schools be governed

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:41.140
in times when the current governing structure is not working?

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.200
What should be done?

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.460
And I think that's an incredibly powerful question, given everything

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:49.180
that we've tried that's worked, based on research over the last 10 years,

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.781
under No Child Left Behind, and Race to the Top.

00:09:51.781 --> 00:09:53.530
But also there's a lot that hasn't worked.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.820
And there's a lot of wasted money and wasted activity.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.510
And so that's one example of how we can work together to say,

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:00.980
this is what we're committed to doing together.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.580
PROFESSOR: Well, so let me be specific about that.

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:07.690
How about the New Orleans model, where every school is a school of choice?

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:12.340
Is that something that should be put into place in other big cities

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:13.875
around the country?

00:10:13.875 --> 00:10:16.000
JOHN WHITE: And we're experiencing this, of course,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.270
in Massachusetts, as we have this dialogue that there

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:22.647
is a referendum on the ballot regarding, are charter schools a good thing?

00:10:22.647 --> 00:10:25.480
And the research in Massachusetts would demonstrate without question

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:26.690
that they are.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.440
In Louisiana, it would demonstrate without question that they are.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.450
And there are other places, though, where charter schools have really

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:32.260
struggled.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:35.110
So it's not just, is an all charter school system a good thing?

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.130
I mean, let's be honest.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.240
Look at Detroit.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.370
It's not working at the scale that it needs

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.600
to work at-- at the quality it needs to work at.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.360
So can it be done well?

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.240
Yes, if there is a plan to have talent, to

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.120
have accountability, to have the right governance model,

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.870
to offer options to families, and to intervene when schools don't do well.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:55.160
If you have those things in place, it can work.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.730
And there's research now in Massachusetts,

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.084
in Louisiana, and elsewhere, to demonstrate that it can work.

00:11:01.084 --> 00:11:02.500
But it's not just charter schools.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:06.070
It's having a plan for who runs them, and what to do when they don't work.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.030
PROFESSOR: No magic bullet is going to solve

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:08.750
the problem of American education.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:09.340
JOHN WHITE: Absolutely not.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.120
No question.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:12.220
PROFESSOR: John, this has been a great opportunity

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:16.012
for me to discuss our schools and where we need to go.

00:11:16.012 --> 00:11:17.720
Thank you very much for joining me today.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.340
JOHN WHITE: Fun for me, as well.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:19.070
Thanks, Paul.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.620
PROFESSOR: Thanks.

